GIVEN `the endorsement is made on a warrant pursuant to subsection (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the peace officer is one to whom the warrant was originally directed, or one within the territorial jurisdiction of the justice by whom it is endorsed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 528(2) the endorsement is sufficient authority to the peace officer to execute the warrant and to take the accused before a justice` MEANS
    IF `the endorsement is made on a warrant pursuant to subsection (1)` AND `the peace officer is one to whom the warrant was originally directed, or one within the territorial jurisdiction of the justice by whom it is endorsed`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "An endorsement that is made on a warrant pursuant to subsection (1) is sufficient authority to the peace officers to whom it was originally directed, and to all peace officers within the territorial jurisdiction of the justice by whom it is endorsed" (another endorsement or another officer is not covered)

@ref s 528(2) the person to be taken before (src:11794)
GIVEN `the justice is the justice who issued the warrant, or another justice for the same territorial division` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 528(2) the justice is one before whom the accused may be taken under the endorsement` MEANS
    IF `the justice is the justice who issued the warrant, or another justice for the same territorial division`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): "before the justice who issued the warrant or before any other justice for the same territorial division" (the text does not say "only")

§ `Sections 529 to 529.5 -- Powers to enter a dwelling-house to carry out an arrest`

-- 529 (1) "A warrant to arrest or apprehend a person issued by a judge or justice under this or any other Act of Parliament may authorize a peace officer, subject to subsection (2), to enter a dwelling-house
-- described in the warrant for the purpose of arresting or apprehending the person if the judge or justice is satisfied by information on oath in writing that there are reasonable grounds to believe that the
-- person is or will be present in the dwelling-house." The keel's `s 2 dwelling-house` has no NOTHING answer, so the place is a BOOLEAN input.
-- src:11798 | 529 (1) A warrant to arrest or apprehend a person issued by a judge or justice under this or any other Act of Parliament may authorize a peace officer, subject to subsection (2), to enter a dwelling-house described in the warrant for the purpose of arresting or apprehending the person if the judge or justice is satisfied by information on oath in writing that there are reasonable grounds to believe that the person is or will be present in the dwelling-house.
@ref s 529(1) (src:11798)
GIVEN `the warrant is a warrant to arrest or apprehend a person issued by a judge or justice under an Act of Parliament` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 2 dwelling-house: the place described in the warrant is one` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the judge or justice is satisfied by information on oath in writing that there are reasonable grounds to believe that the person is or will be present in the dwelling-house` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 529(1) the warrant may authorize a peace officer to enter a dwelling-house described in the warrant for the purpose of arresting or apprehending the person` MEANS
    IF `the warrant is a warrant to arrest or apprehend a person issued by a judge or justice under an Act of Parliament`
        AND `s 2 dwelling-house: the place described in the warrant is one`
        AND `the judge or justice is satisfied by information on oath in writing that there are reasonable grounds to believe that the person is or will be present in the dwelling-house`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "may authorize a peace officer ... to enter a dwelling-house described in the warrant ... if the judge or justice is satisfied by information on oath in writing that there are reasonable grounds to believe that the person is or will be present in the dwelling-house" (a "may" whose "if" gate fails; the other reading is JUST FALSE)

-- 529 (2) "An authorization to enter a dwelling-house granted under subsection (1) is subject to the condition that the peace officer may not enter the dwelling-house unless the peace officer has, immediately
-- before entering the dwelling-house, reasonable grounds to believe that the person to be arrested or apprehended is present in the dwelling-house."
-- src:11800 | (2) An authorization to enter a dwelling-house granted under subsection (1) is subject to the condition that the peace officer may not enter the dwelling-house unless the peace officer has, immediately before entering the dwelling-house, reasonable grounds to believe that the person to be arrested or apprehended is present in the dwelling-house.
@ref s 529(2) (src:11800)
GIVEN `an authorization to enter a dwelling-house has been granted under subsection (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the peace officer has, immediately before entering the dwelling-house, reasonable grounds to believe that the person to be arrested or apprehended is present in the dwelling-house` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 529(2) the peace officer is barred from entering the dwelling-house` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `an authorization to enter a dwelling-house has been granted under subsection (1)` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "An authorization to enter a dwelling-house granted under subsection (1) is subject to the condition" (the bar is for an authorization granted under subsection (1))
        IF `the peace officer has, immediately before entering the dwelling-house, reasonable grounds to believe that the person to be arrested or apprehended is present in the dwelling-house` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the peace officer may not enter the dwelling-house unless the peace officer has, immediately before entering the dwelling-house, reasonable grounds to believe that the person to be arrested or apprehended is present in the dwelling-house"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- 529.1 "A judge or justice may issue a warrant in Form 7.1 authorizing a peace officer to enter a dwelling-house described in the warrant for the purpose of arresting or apprehending a person identified or
-- identifiable by the warrant if the judge or justice is satisfied by information on oath that there are reasonable grounds to believe that the person is or will be present in the dwelling-house and that (a) a
-- warrant referred to in this or any other Act of Parliament to arrest or apprehend the person is in force anywhere in Canada; (b) grounds exist to arrest the person without warrant under paragraph 495(1)(a) or (b)
-- or section 672.91; or (c) grounds exist to arrest or apprehend without warrant the person under an Act of Parliament, other than this Act."
-- src:11802 | 529.1 A judge or justice may issue a warrant in Form 7.1 authorizing a peace officer to enter a dwelling-house described in the warrant for the purpose of arresting or apprehending a person identified or identifiable by the warrant if the judge or justice is satisfied by information on oath that there are reasonable grounds to believe that the person is or will be present in the dwelling-house and that
-- src:11803 | (a) a warrant referred to in this or any other Act of Parliament to arrest or apprehend the person is in force anywhere in Canada;
-- src:11804 | (b) grounds exist to arrest the person without warrant under paragraph 495(1)(a) or (b) or section 672.91; or
-- src:11805 | (c) grounds exist to arrest or apprehend without warrant the person under an Act of Parliament, other than this Act.
@ref s 529.1 (src:11802-11805)
GIVEN `the judge or justice is satisfied by information on oath that there are reasonable grounds to believe that the person is or will be present in the dwelling-house` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a warrant referred to in this or any other Act of Parliament to arrest or apprehend the person is in force anywhere in Canada` IS A BOOLEAN
      `grounds exist to arrest the person without warrant under paragraph 495(1)(a) or (b) or section 672.91` IS A BOOLEAN
      `grounds exist to arrest or apprehend without warrant the person under an Act of Parliament, other than this Act` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 529.1 the judge or justice may issue a warrant in Form 7.1 authorizing a peace officer to enter a dwelling-house described in the warrant` MEANS
    IF `the judge or justice is satisfied by information on oath that there are reasonable grounds to believe that the person is or will be present in the dwelling-house`
        AND (`a warrant referred to in this or any other Act of Parliament to arrest or apprehend the person is in force anywhere in Canada`
             OR `grounds exist to arrest the person without warrant under paragraph 495(1)(a) or (b) or section 672.91`
             OR `grounds exist to arrest or apprehend without warrant the person under an Act of Parliament, other than this Act`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "may issue a warrant in Form 7.1 ... if the judge or justice is satisfied by information on oath that there are reasonable grounds to believe that the person is or will be present in the dwelling-house and that (a) ...; (b) ...; or (c) ..." (a "may" whose "if" gate fails; the other reading is JUST FALSE)

-- 529.2 "Subject to section 529.4, the judge or justice shall include in a warrant referred to in section 529 or 529.1 any terms and conditions that the judge or justice considers advisable to ensure that the entry
-- into the dwelling-house is reasonable in the circumstances." "Subject to section 529.4" is not computed: section 529.4 is about announcement, and this duty is read beside it.
-- src:11807 | 529.2 Subject to section 529.4, the judge or justice shall include in a warrant referred to in section 529 or 529.1 any terms and conditions that the judge or justice considers advisable to ensure that the entry into the dwelling-house is reasonable in the circumstances.
@ref s 529.2 (src:11807)
GIVEN `the warrant is a warrant referred to in section 529 or 529.1` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the term or condition is one that the judge or justice considers advisable to ensure that the entry into the dwelling-house is reasonable in the circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 529.2 the judge or justice must include the term or condition in the warrant` MEANS
    IF `the warrant is a warrant referred to in section 529 or 529.1` AND `the term or condition is one that the judge or justice considers advisable to ensure that the entry into the dwelling-house is reasonable in the circumstances`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the judge or justice shall include in a warrant referred to in section 529 or 529.1 any terms and conditions that the judge or justice considers advisable to ensure that the entry into the dwelling-house is reasonable in the circumstances"

-- 529.3 (1) "Without limiting or restricting any power a peace officer may have to enter a dwelling-house under this or any other Act or law, the peace officer may enter the dwelling-house for the purpose of
-- arresting or apprehending a person, without a warrant referred to in section 529 or 529.1 authorizing the entry, if the peace officer has reasonable grounds to believe that the person is present in the
-- dwelling-house, and the conditions for obtaining a warrant under section 529.1 exist but by reason of exigent circumstances it would be impracticable to obtain a warrant."
-- src:11809 | 529.3 (1) Without limiting or restricting any power a peace officer may have to enter a dwelling-house under this or any other Act or law, the peace officer may enter the dwelling-house for the purpose of arresting or apprehending a person, without a warrant referred to in section 529 or 529.1 authorizing the entry, if the peace officer has reasonable grounds to believe that the person is present in the dwelling-house, and the conditions for obtaining a warrant under section 529.1 exist but by reason of exigent circumstances it would be impracticable to obtain a warrant.
@ref s 529.3(1) (src:11809)
GIVEN `the peace officer has reasonable grounds to believe that the person is present in the dwelling-house` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the conditions for obtaining a warrant under section 529.1 exist` IS A BOOLEAN
      `by reason of exigent circumstances it would be impracticable to obtain a warrant` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 529.3(1) the peace officer may enter the dwelling-house for the purpose of arresting or apprehending a person without a warrant` MEANS
    IF `the peace officer has reasonable grounds to believe that the person is present in the dwelling-house`
        AND `the conditions for obtaining a warrant under section 529.1 exist`
        AND `by reason of exigent circumstances it would be impracticable to obtain a warrant`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "the peace officer may enter the dwelling-house ... without a warrant ... if the peace officer has reasonable grounds to believe that the person is present in the dwelling-house, and the conditions for obtaining a warrant under section 529.1 exist but by reason of exigent circumstances it would be impracticable to obtain a warrant" (a "may" whose "if" gate fails; the other reading is JUST FALSE; the opening words keep every other power the officer may have)

-- 529.3 (2) "For the purposes of subsection (1), exigent circumstances include circumstances in which the peace officer (a) has reasonable grounds to suspect that entry into the dwelling-house is necessary to
-- prevent imminent bodily harm or death to any person; or (b) has reasonable grounds to believe that evidence relating to the commission of an indictable offence is present in the dwelling-house and that entry
-- into the dwelling-house is necessary to prevent the imminent loss or imminent destruction of the evidence."
-- src:11811 | (2) For the purposes of subsection (1), exigent circumstances include circumstances in which the peace officer
-- src:11812 | (a) has reasonable grounds to suspect that entry into the dwelling-house is necessary to prevent imminent bodily harm or death to any person; or
-- src:11813 | (b) has reasonable grounds to believe that evidence relating to the commission of an indictable offence is present in the dwelling-house and that entry into the dwelling-house is necessary to prevent the imminent loss or imminent destruction of the evidence.
@ref s 529.3(2) (src:11811-11813)
GIVEN `the peace officer has reasonable grounds to suspect that entry into the dwelling-house is necessary to prevent imminent bodily harm or death to any person` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the peace officer has reasonable grounds to believe that evidence relating to the commission of an offence is present in the dwelling-house and that entry into the dwelling-house is necessary to prevent the imminent loss or imminent destruction of the evidence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the offence the evidence relates to` IS A `s 493.01 the offence charged`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 529.3(2) the circumstances are exigent circumstances` MEANS
    IF `the peace officer has reasonable grounds to suspect that entry into the dwelling-house is necessary to prevent imminent bodily harm or death to any person`
        OR (`the peace officer has reasonable grounds to believe that evidence relating to the commission of an offence is present in the dwelling-house and that entry into the dwelling-house is necessary to prevent the imminent loss or imminent destruction of the evidence`
            AND `s 493.01 the offence is treated as an indictable offence` `the offence the evidence relates to`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "exigent circumstances include circumstances in which the peace officer" (the list does not say "only")

-- 529.4 (1) "A judge or justice who authorizes a peace officer to enter a dwelling-house under section 529 or 529.1, or any other judge or justice, may authorize the peace officer to enter the dwelling-house
-- without prior announcement if the judge or justice is satisfied by information on oath that there are reasonable grounds to believe that prior announcement of the entry would (a) expose the peace officer or any
-- other person to imminent bodily harm or death; or (b) result in the imminent loss or imminent destruction of evidence relating to the commission of an indictable offence."
-- src:11815 | 529.4 (1) A judge or justice who authorizes a peace officer to enter a dwelling-house under section 529 or 529.1, or any other judge or justice, may authorize the peace officer to enter the dwelling-house without prior announcement if the judge or justice is satisfied by information on oath that there are reasonable grounds to believe that prior announcement of the entry would
-- src:11816 | (a) expose the peace officer or any other person to imminent bodily harm or death; or
-- src:11817 | (b) result in the imminent loss or imminent destruction of evidence relating to the commission of an indictable offence.
@ref s 529.4(1) (src:11815-11817)
GIVEN `the judge or justice is satisfied by information on oath that there are reasonable grounds to believe that prior announcement of the entry would expose the peace officer or any other person to imminent bodily harm or death` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the judge or justice is satisfied by information on oath that there are reasonable grounds to believe that prior announcement of the entry would result in the imminent loss or imminent destruction of evidence relating to the commission of an offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the offence the evidence relates to` IS A `s 493.01 the offence charged`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 529.4(1) the judge or justice may authorize the peace officer to enter the dwelling-house without prior announcement` MEANS
    IF `the judge or justice is satisfied by information on oath that there are reasonable grounds to believe that prior announcement of the entry would expose the peace officer or any other person to imminent bodily harm or death`
        OR (`the judge or justice is satisfied by information on oath that there are reasonable grounds to believe that prior announcement of the entry would result in the imminent loss or imminent destruction of evidence relating to the commission of an offence`
            AND `s 493.01 the offence is treated as an indictable offence` `the offence the evidence relates to`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "may authorize the peace officer to enter the dwelling-house without prior announcement if the judge or justice is satisfied by information on oath that there are reasonable grounds to believe that prior announcement of the entry would (a) ...; or (b) ..." (a "may" whose "if" gate fails; the other reading is JUST FALSE)

-- 529.4 (2) "An authorization under this section is subject to the condition that the peace officer may not enter the dwelling-house without prior announcement despite being authorized to do so unless the peace
-- officer has, immediately before entering the dwelling-house, (a) reasonable grounds to suspect that prior announcement of the entry would expose the peace officer or any other person to imminent bodily harm or
-- death; or (b) reasonable grounds to believe that prior announcement of the entry would result in the imminent loss or imminent destruction of evidence relating to the commission of an indictable offence."
-- src:11819 | (2) An authorization under this section is subject to the condition that the peace officer may not enter the dwelling-house without prior announcement despite being authorized to do so unless the peace officer has, immediately before entering the dwelling-house,
-- src:11820 | (a) reasonable grounds to suspect that prior announcement of the entry would expose the peace officer or any other person to imminent bodily harm or death; or
-- src:11821 | (b) reasonable grounds to believe that prior announcement of the entry would result in the imminent loss or imminent destruction of evidence relating to the commission of an indictable offence.
@ref s 529.4(2) (src:11819-11821)
GIVEN `an authorization has been granted under this section` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the peace officer has, immediately before entering the dwelling-house, reasonable grounds to suspect that prior announcement of the entry would expose the peace officer or any other person to imminent bodily harm or death` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the peace officer has, immediately before entering the dwelling-house, reasonable grounds to believe that prior announcement of the entry would result in the imminent loss or imminent destruction of evidence relating to the commission of an offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the offence the evidence relates to` IS A `s 493.01 the offence charged`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 529.4(2) the peace officer is barred from entering the dwelling-house without prior announcement despite being authorized to do so` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `an authorization has been granted under this section` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "An authorization under this section is subject to the condition" (the bar is for an authorization under this section)
        IF `the peace officer has, immediately before entering the dwelling-house, reasonable grounds to suspect that prior announcement of the entry would expose the peace officer or any other person to imminent bodily harm or death`
            THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "may not enter the dwelling-house without prior announcement despite being authorized to do so unless the peace officer has, immediately before entering the dwelling-house, (a) reasonable grounds to suspect that prior announcement of the entry would expose the peace officer or any other person to imminent bodily harm or death"
        IF `the peace officer has, immediately before entering the dwelling-house, reasonable grounds to believe that prior announcement of the entry would result in the imminent loss or imminent destruction of evidence relating to the commission of an offence`
            AND `s 493.01 the offence is treated as an indictable offence` `the offence the evidence relates to`
            THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless the peace officer has, immediately before entering the dwelling-house, ... (b) reasonable grounds to believe that prior announcement of the entry would result in the imminent loss or imminent destruction of evidence relating to the commission of an indictable offence"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- 529.4 (3) "A peace officer who enters a dwelling-house without a warrant under section 529.3 may not enter the dwelling-house without prior announcement unless the peace officer has, immediately before entering
-- the dwelling-house, (a) reasonable grounds to suspect that prior announcement of the entry would expose the peace officer or any other person to imminent bodily harm or death; or (b) reasonable grounds to believe
-- that prior announcement of the entry would result in the imminent loss or imminent destruction of evidence relating to the commission of an indictable offence."
